          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I feel sisterly love for you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Jane’s loving emotional state toward St John. The immediate qualification “as a sister” identifies it as familial rather than romantic love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal does not itself promise marriage, travel, missionary service, or any future conduct. Jane instead explicitly denies being obligated; her separate, earlier offer to serve as an assistant must not be transferred onto “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is embedded in a reasoned explanation of Jane’s position and functions as an avowal, not as a reflex-like or uncontrolled discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression’s principal force is adequately represented by T. Its conciliatory and contrastive role—affirming sisterly affection while rejecting marriage and obligation—does not constitute a distinct core mode beyond the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is disputing St John’s claim that she has promised or engaged to go to India. She explains that sisterly affection, admiration, and confidence would have made her willing to risk much with him, while denying any obligation and maintaining her refusal to become his wife.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal “I love you” undertakes nothing. Jane’s separate offer to accompany St John as an assistant has been rejected by him as unsuitable unless she becomes his wife; she refuses marriage and expressly denies that she made a binding promise or engagement. Thus there is no mutually accepted undertaking to travel together.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the supplied passages, with prior familiarity, as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports genuine sisterly or friendly affection in this represented moment, but not romantic love. It does not independently settle every aspect of Jane’s broader feelings or St John’s interpretation beyond what the supplied passages state.\"}"
        }
